  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a literature review of academic publications rather than an empirical assessment of deployed municipal transportation systems.",
      "what": "This systematic review analyzed 2,253 studies published between 2021 and 2026 to evaluate disability-inclusive intelligent transportation systems (ITS). The analysis reveals that 66.0% of the literature focuses on general accessibility frameworks, while only 10.2% addresses cognitive impairments and 5.5% targets mobility impairments. Most proposed AI and deep learning solutions remain simulation-based and lack real-world validation, standardized evaluation metrics, and accessibility-aware datasets.",
      "why": "It exposes a critical disconnect in smart-city research where advanced AI mobility models are rarely tested in real-world scenarios or designed for specific, high-need disability groups, providing a unified framework to bridge technology, policy, and user-centered design."
    }
  },
